{"results":[{"url":"https://www.citizensadvice.org.uk/benefits/universal-credit/if-youre-sick-or-disabled/getting-universal-credit-if-youre-sick-or-disabled/","final_url":"https://www.citizensadvice.org.uk/benefits/universal-credit/if-youre-sick-or-disabled/getting-universal-credit-if-youre-sick-or-disabled/","title":"Getting Universal Credit if you’re sick or disabled","description":"Check if you can apply for support if you can't work because you're sick or disabled. Universal Credit is available for people with sickness or disabilities.","language":"en","text":"# Getting Universal Credit if you’re sick or disabled\n\nThis advice applies to England. See advice for See advice for Northern Ireland, See advice for Scotland, See advice for Wales\n\n## Help us improve our website\n\nTake 5 minutes to tell us if you found what you needed on our website. Your feedback will help us give millions of people the information they need.\n\nIf you have difficulty working because you’re sick or disabled, you might:\n\n* get more Universal Credit\n* not have to look for work while you get Universal Credit\n\nIf the Department for Work and Pensions (DWP) agree you can’t work, they’ll check if you can get ready to work in the future.\n\n## If the DWP think you can get ready to work\n\nThey’ll say you have 'limited capability for work' (LCW).\n\nYou won’t have to look for work. You might have to do tasks to get ready for work - like training or writing a CV. \n\nYou won't usually get extra money if you have LCW.\n\n## If the DWP don’t think you can get ready for work\n\nThey’ll say you have 'limited capability for work-related activity' (LCWRA).\n\nYou won’t have to work or do anything to get ready for work. You’ll get an additional payment in your Universal Credit called the ‘LCWRA element’.\n\nThe payment is either a higher amount of £429.80 a month or a lower amount of £217.26 a month. The amount you get depends on your circumstances.\n\nIf you started getting the LCWRA element before 6 April 2026, you’ll get the higher amount.\n\nIf you reported a health condition or disability and asked for a health assessment before 6 April and the DWP decide you have LCWRA, you’ll get the higher amount.\n\nIf you report your health condition or disability on or after 6 April and the DWP decide you have LCWRA, you’ll only get the higher amount if:\n\n* the DWP say you also meet their ‘severe conditions’ criteria\n* you’re terminally ill and your health professional says you might not live more than a year\n* you started getting the support component of the Employment and Support Allowance (ESA) before 6 April, and continue to get it until you claim Universal Credit\n\nIf none of these apply, you’ll get the lower amount.\n\n### Check if you meet the ‘severe conditions’ criteria\n\nThe DWP will assess how difficult you find certain everyday activities to decide if you have LCW or LCWRA. They’ll use statements called ‘descriptors’ to describe how difficult you find certain tasks.\n\nSome descriptors automatically mean you have LCWRA - for example, if you can’t walk more than 50 metres without help, or do a simple task like setting an alarm clock.\n\nIf you report your health condition or disability on or after 6 April 2026, the DWP will also use these descriptors to decide if you meet their ‘severe conditions’ criteria.\n\nFor you to meet the ‘severe conditions’ criteria, at least 1 of the automatic LCWRA descriptors must apply to you in all these ways:\n\n* at all times, or whenever you try to do the activity described by the descriptor\n* permanently, for the rest of your life\n* because of a medical condition or disability diagnosed by the NHS\n\nYou can check the descriptors for different types of health condition or disability.\n\n### Check when you’ll start getting the LCWRA element\n\nIf the DWP say you have LCWRA, there’s usually a 3 month wait before the LCWRA element is added to your Universal Credit. It becomes part of your Universal Credit payment in the assessment period after this 3 month period finishes. An assessment period is the monthly period the DWP uses to work out your Universal Credit.\n\nThere are some situations where you might get the LCWRA element straight away - for example:\n\n* if you’re terminally ill and your health professional says you might not live more than a year\n* if you get new style Employment and Support Allowance (ESA) with a support or work-related activity component\n\nYou can check the full list of reasons why you might get the LCWRA element earlier - use the section ‘If you’re sick or disabled’. You can also talk to an adviser.\n\n## If you’re moving to Universal Credit from other benefits\n\nUniversal Credit is replacing 6 benefits called ‘legacy benefits’. These are:\n\n* Housing Benefit\n* income-related Employment and Support Allowance (ESA)\n* income-based Jobseeker’s Allowance (JSA)\n* Child Tax Credits (CTC)\n* Working Tax Credits (WTC)\n* Income Support\n\nMost people’s legacy benefits have ended. You’ll need to move to Universal Credit if you get a letter telling you to claim by a certain date. This is called a 'migration notice'.\n\nSome people can continue to get income-related ESA or Housing Benefit - but only in certain situations. Check if you should move to Universal Credit.\n\n### If you were getting a benefit with a severe disability premium (SDP)\n\nYou might get an extra amount in your Universal Credit if you were getting, or recently stopped getting, a benefit with a severe disability premium (SDP). The extra amount isn't called an SDP - it's called a 'transitional element’.\n\nWhat you need to do depends on whether you’ve had a migration notice.\n\n#### If you’ve had a migration notice\n\nThe DWP might add an extra amount to your Universal Credit called a ‘managed migration transitional element’. This is to stop you being worse off than you were on your old benefits. This will usually take the SDP into account and other premiums you used to get.\n\nYou might not get a managed migration transitional element if:\n\n* your old benefits have already stopped\n* you get more money from Universal Credit than your old benefits\n\nIf you don’t get the managed migration transitional element in your Universal Credit, you might still get an extra amount because you used to get the SDP. This is called the ‘SDP transitional element’. The amount you get will depend on a few things, such as how much you were being awarded in your legacy benefits. Check how much Universal Credit you’ll get - check the section about other amounts you can get.\n\nYou’ll need to apply for Universal Credit within a month after you stop getting the benefit with the SDP. You can’t get the SDP transitional element if you:\n\n* were only getting the SDP with Housing Benefit\n* move in with a partner who is claiming Universal Credit\n* stop being eligible for the SDP\n\nIf you get a transitional element, the DWP will reduce this over time - so eventually you’ll just get what you normally would on Universal Credit.\n\nIf you’re not sure when to apply for Universal Credit, talk to an adviser.\n\n#### If you haven’t had a migration notice\n\nTo get the extra amount, you’ll need to apply for Universal Credit within a month after you stop getting the benefit with the SDP. The extra amount is called the ‘SDP transitional element’. The amount you get will depend on a few things, such as how much you were being awarded in your legacy benefit. Check how much Universal Credit you’ll get - check the section about other amounts you can get.\n\nYou can’t get the SDP transitional element if you:\n\n* were only getting the SDP with Housing Benefit\n* move in with a partner who is claiming Universal Credit\n* stop being eligible for the SDP\n\nIf you get a transitional element, the DWP will reduce this over time - so eventually you’ll just get what you normally would on Universal Credit.\n\n## Tell the DWP you have difficulty working\n\nIt’s important to show you have LCW or LCWRA as soon as possible. This is very important if you think you have LCWRA because it might mean you're entitled to more money.\n\nYou’ll need to send the DWP a ‘fit note’ from your doctor. The fit note confirms your disability or health condition to the DWP. You might be able to provide a different type of evidence, for example a hospital discharge letter.\n\nYou don’t need a fit note for the first 7 days of being unfit for work. You can ‘self-certify’ - this means telling the DWP in writing that you can’t work because of your health condition or disability.\n\nTell the DWP as soon as possible so they know when you started to find it difficult to work.\n\nIf you're making a new claim, use the application form to tell the DWP about your disability or health condition. You should explain how the disability or condition makes it hard for you to work.\n\nIf you're already getting Universal Credit, you should use the ‘Report a change of circumstances’ tab on your online account. If you don’t have an online account, you can call the Universal Credit helpline.\n\nUniversal Credit helpline\n\nTelephone: 0800 328 5644\n\nTelephone (Welsh language): 0800 328 1744\n\nTextphone: 0800 328 1344\n\nRelay UK - if you can't hear or speak on the phone, you can type what you want to say: 18001 then 0800 328 5644\n\nYou can use Relay UK with an app or a textphone. There’s no extra charge to use it. Find out how to use Relay UK on the Relay UK website.\n\nVideo relay - if you use British Sign Language (BSL).\n\nYou can find out how to use video relay on YouTube.\n\nMonday to Friday, 8am to 6pm\n\nCalls are free from mobiles and landlines.\n\n### How to get a fit note\n\nYou’ll need to get a fit note if you’re still unable to work after the first 7 days.\n\nYou can get a fit note from your:\n\n* GP or another doctor\n* registered nurse\n* chemist\n* occupational therapist\n* physiotherapist\n\nYour fit note will be either printed or digital. If you’re not sure which kind you’ll get and how you’ll get it, check with the healthcare professional.\n\nIf you get a printed fit note, check that the healthcare professional has signed it.\n\nIf you get a digital fit note, check that it includes the healthcare professional’s name.\n\nIf the healthcare professional hasn't either signed your fit note or included their name, it could be rejected by the DWP and you might have to get a new one.\n\nYou should always keep your fit note - you might have to pay for a replacement if you lose or delete it.\n\nIt’s a good idea to add your fit note to your online account. If you have a printed fit note, you’ll need to take a photo of it to upload. If you can’t upload the fit note, write a message in your journal to explain the problem to the DWP.\n\nIf you can’t get a fit note, talk to an adviser.\n\n## Check if you can get LCW or LCWRA automatically\n\nTo get LCW or LCWRA, you’ll usually need to fill in another form. This is called the ‘Capability for Work questionnaire’ or ‘WCA50’ form. You'll also usually need to go to a medical assessment. This process usually takes a few months.\n\nIn some situations you might LCW or LCWRA automatically - if you.\n\n* are pregnant\n* were getting Employment and Support Allowance (ESA) when you applied for Universal Credit\n* are over State Pension age and getting other benefits\n* are in hospital or quarantine\n* are having cancer treatment, or recovering from it\n* have a serious or terminal illness\n\nThis might mean you do not need to fill in a WCA50 form and have an assessment. This depends on your health condition or disability and how it affects you.\n\n### You're pregnant\n\nYou should get LCWRA automatically if working or getting ready for work could be a risk to your health or your baby's health. Tell your work coach and make a note on your online account.\n\nYou’ll need to wait 3 months to get the LCWRA element. If you already do some work-related requirements, the DWP might decide you can stop doing them before 3 months.\n\nIf you have any other health conditions or disabilities, you should still complete a WCA50 form to tell the DWP about them.\n\nYou should also use the form to tell them if you think you might meet the ‘severe conditions’ criteria. If you’re not sure if you meet the criteria, you can talk to an adviser.\n\nThe DWP might send you a form. If they don’t, you can ask for one - call the Universal Credit helpline or request it through your online account.\n\nUniversal Credit helpline\n\nTelephone: 0800 328 5644\n\nTelephone (Welsh language): 0800 328 1744\n\nTextphone: 0800 328 1344\n\nRelay UK - if you can't hear or speak on the phone, you can type what you want to say: 18001 then 0800 328 5644\n\nYou can use Relay UK with an app or a textphone. There’s no extra charge to use it. Find out how to use Relay UK on the Relay UK website.\n\nVideo relay - if you use British Sign Language (BSL).\n\nYou can find out how to use video relay on YouTube.\n\nMonday to Friday, 8am to 6pm\n\nCalls are free from mobiles and landlines.\n\n### You get Employment and Support Allowance (ESA)\n\nIf you get the ESA support component, you'll get the LCWRA element straight away.\n\nYou might still need to fill in the WCA50 form and go to an assessment - it depends which type of ESA you get.\n\n#### If you get new style ESA\n\nIf you get the ESA support component, you'll automatically have LCWRA.\n\nYou’ll get the higher amount of the LCWRA element if you started getting the support component before 6 April 2026, and continue getting it until the day your UC award starts.\n\nOtherwise, you’ll only get the higher amount of the LCWRA element if either of these apply to you:\n\n* the DWP say you also meet their ‘severe conditions’ criteria\n* you’re terminally ill and your health professional says you might not live more than a year\n\nIf you’re in the ESA work-related activity group, you’ll automatically have LCW.\n\nIf you already have LCW or LCWRA for new style ESA, you don't need to fill in a form or have a medical assessment to get LCW or LCWRA for Universal Credit.\n\nIf you might meet the ‘severe conditions’ criteria, you might need to fill in a form and have a new assessment to show this. If you’re not sure if you meet the criteria, you can talk to an adviser.\n\n#### If you got old-style ESA\n\nYou can get LCW or LCWRA straight away if you:\n\n* were getting ESA in the support group or the work-related activity group\n* claimed Universal Credit before you stopped being eligible for ESA\n\nYou'll still have LCW or LCWRA on Universal Credit as long as there's no break between your old-style ESA claim and your Universal Credit claim.\n\nIf you were in the support group, you’ll get the higher amount of the LCWRA element.\n\nThe DWP might send you a WCA50 form to check you’re still eligible for LCW or LCWRA.\n\n### You're over State Pension age and getting other benefits\n\nYou'll get LCWRA automatically if you're over State Pension age and getting one of the following disability benefits:\n\n* Attendance Allowance\n* the enhanced rate of the daily living component of Personal Independence Payment (PIP)\n* the highest rate of the care component of Disability Living Allowance (DLA)\n* the enhanced rate of the daily living component of Adult Disability Payment\n* highest rate Scottish Adult Disability Living Allowance care component\n* Pension Age Disability Payment\n\nYou'll get LCW automatically if you're getting either:\n\n* PIP, but not the enhanced daily living component\n* DLA, but not the highest rate care component\n* Adult Disability Payment or Scottish Adult Disability Living Allowance, but not the enhanced rate of the daily living component\n\n#### If you were previously getting Employment and Support Allowance (ESA)\n\nYou’ll get LCW or LCWRA automatically if:\n\n* you got ESA in the past\n* you stopped qualifying for ESA because you reached State Pension age\n* you claimed Universal Credit within a month of your ESA ending\n\nIf you were in the ESA support group, you’ll automatically get LCWRA.\n\nIf you were in the ESA work-related activity group, you’ll automatically get LCW.\n\n#### If the DWP don't send you a WCA50 form\n\nIf you automatically get LCW, the DWP should send you a WCA50 form to check if you should be getting LCWRA instead.\n\nIf you automatically get LCWRA, they should send you a WCA50 form to check if you meet the ‘severe conditions’ criteria.\n\nIf they don’t send you a WCA50 form, call the Universal Credit helpline or request it through your online account.\n\nUniversal Credit helpline\n\nTelephone: 0800 328 5644\n\nTelephone (Welsh language): 0800 328 1744\n\nTextphone: 0800 328 1344\n\nRelay UK - if you can't hear or speak on the phone, you can type what you want to say: 18001 then 0800 328 5644\n\nYou can use Relay UK with an app or a textphone. There’s no extra charge to use it. Find out how to use Relay UK on the Relay UK website.\n\nVideo relay - if you use British Sign Language (BSL).\n\nYou can find out how to use video relay on YouTube.\n\nMonday to Friday, 8am to 6pm\n\nCalls are free from mobiles and landlines.\n\n### You're in hospital or quarantine\n\nIf you go into hospital or drug or alcohol rehab for at least 24 hours, update your Universal Credit online account as soon as you can.\n\nYou'll have LCW while you're in hospital. You'll also have LCW after you come out, if the DWP agree you need time to recover.\n\nYou'll also have LCW if you've been given official notice not to work because you have an infectious disease.\n\nThe DWP should also send you a WCA50 form to check if you should be getting LCWRA. If they don’t, call the Universal Credit helpline or request it through your online account.\n\nUniversal Credit helpline\n\nTelephone: 0800 328 5644\n\nTelephone (Welsh language): 0800 328 1744\n\nTextphone: 0800 328 1344\n\nRelay UK - if you can't hear or speak on the phone, you can type what you want to say: 18001 then 0800 328 5644\n\nYou can use Relay UK with an app or a textphone. There’s no extra charge to use it. Find out how to use Relay UK on the Relay UK website.\n\nVideo relay - if you use British Sign Language (BSL).\n\nYou can find out how to use video relay on YouTube.\n\nMonday to Friday, 8am to 6pm\n\nCalls are free from mobiles and landlines.\n\n### You’re having cancer treatment, or recovering from it\n\nYou're likely to have LCWRA if you're:\n\n* being treated with chemotherapy or radiotherapy\n* likely to start treatment in the next 6 months\n* recovering from treatment\n\nYou might also have LCWRA if you're getting other cancer treatments - like immunotherapy, biological treatment or hormonal treatment. If you're being treated with any of these and the DWP decide you don't have LCWRA, talk to an adviser.\n\nThe DWP will usually still send you a WCA50 form to complete. This includes a section on cancer treatment. If you have any other conditions or disabilities, you should tell the DWP about these on the form. You should also use the form to tell them if you think you might meet the ‘severe conditions’ criteria. If you’re not sure if you meet the criteria, you can talk to an adviser.\n\nYou’ll usually need to wait 3 months to get the LCWRA element.\n\n#### If you have a terminal illness and might not live more than a year\n\nYou'll always have LCWRA and get the higher amount. You can stop doing work-related requirements and get the LCWRA element straight away.\n\nYou don’t need to complete a WCA50 form. You’ll need to send the DWP a medical report from a doctor or another health professional. The report will be on form SR1. You don’t have to pay for it.\n\nAsk your doctor or another healthcare professional to email the report to the DWP to save time.\n\n### You have a serious or terminal illness\n\nThere are some treatments and conditions where you'll get LCW or LCWRA automatically. If you have LCWRA, you might need to wait 3 months to get the LCWRA element.\n\nYou'll still need to put a note on your online account mentioning your diagnosis and explaining how your illness or treatment stops you working or preparing for work.\n\nYou’ll have LCW if you're having:\n\n* weekly dialysis\n* plasma exchange\n* intravenous feeding\n\nYour LCW will continue if you've finished one of these treatments and the DWP agree you need time to recover.\n\nThe DWP should send you a WCA50 form. This is to check if you have LCWRA - and if you do, to check if you meet their ‘severe conditions’ criteria to get the higher amount.\n\nIf the DWP don't send you a WCA50 form, call the Universal Credit helpline or request it through your online account.\n\nUniversal Credit helpline\n\nTelephone: 0800 328 5644\n\nTelephone (Welsh language): 0800 328 1744\n\nTextphone: 0800 328 1344\n\nRelay UK - if you can't hear or speak on the phone, you can type what you want to say: 18001 then 0800 328 5644\n\nYou can use Relay UK with an app or a textphone. There’s no extra charge to use it. Find out how to use Relay UK on the Relay UK website.\n\nVideo relay - if you use British Sign Language (BSL).\n\nYou can find out how to use video relay on YouTube.\n\nMonday to Friday, 8am to 6pm\n\nCalls are free from mobiles and landlines.\n\n#### If you have a terminal illness and might not live more than a year\n\nYou'll always have LCWRA and be paid the higher amount. You can stop doing work-related requirements and get the LCWRA element straight away.\n\nYou don’t need to complete a WCA50 form. You’ll need to send the DWP a medical report from your doctor or another health professional. The report will be on form SR1. You don’t have to pay for it.\n\nAsk your GP or consultant to email the report to the DWP to save time.\n\n## If you think you should have got LCW or LCWRA automatically\n\nIf you think you should have got LCW or LCWRA automatically without the form or assessment, you can ask the DWP to change their decision.\n\n## Get ready to fill in the work capability form\n\nIf you need to fill in a WCA50 form, there are things you can do to prepare - such as getting medical evidence and keeping a diary of your health. Check how to prepare to complete the WCA50 form.\n\n## Help us improve our website\n\nTake 5 minutes to tell us if you found what you needed on our website. Your feedback will help us give millions of people the information they need.\n\nPage last reviewed on 06 April 2026","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":787.6288659990678,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}